## Overview

ASP.NET Core ships with a **built‑in, low‑overhead dependency injection (DI) container** that is fully integrated into the application pipeline.  
The framework does three things for you:

1. **Collects all the services you register** (in `Startup.ConfigureServices` or the newer `Program.cs`).
2. **Builds a single `IServiceProvider`** once during host construction.
3. **Wires that provider into every part of the pipeline** – MVC controllers, Razor Pages, middleware, background workers, minimal‑API handlers, etc.

Because the container is built into the framework, you usually **never have to instantiate it yourself**. You just tell it *what* you need, and it takes care of *how* to provide it, recursively, on a per‑request basis.

---

## 1. Registering Services

All registrations happen against an `IServiceCollection`.  
Typical place: `Program.cs` (minimal APIs) or `Startup.ConfigureServices`.

```csharp
// Program.cs (minimal API)
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// 1. Add framework services
builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews();   // MVC
builder.Services.AddRazorPages();             // Razor Pages
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();   // Swagger
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();

// 2. Register your own services
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IEmailSender, SmtpEmailSender>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IUserRepository, UserRepository>();
builder.Services.AddTransient<IPaymentGateway, StripeGateway>();

// 3. Register EF Core context (scoped)
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

// 4. Configure options
builder.Services.Configure<StripeOptions>(
    builder.Configuration.GetSection("Stripe"));

// 5. Register HttpClient factory
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IWeatherService, WeatherService>();

var app = builder.Build();
```

### Registration syntax

| Lifetime | Method | Example |
|----------|--------|---------|
| **Singleton** | `AddSingleton<Interface, Implementation>()` | One instance for the entire app. |
| **Scoped** | `AddScoped<Interface, Implementation>()` | One instance per *HTTP request* (or per custom scope). |
| **Transient** | `AddTransient<Interface, Implementation>()` | New instance each time it is requested. |

You can also register **instances** or **factory lambdas**:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<MyService>(new MyService("static config"));
builder.Services.AddTransient<IMyService>(sp => new MyService(sp.GetRequiredService<ILogger<MyService>>()));
```

---

## 2. Service Lifetimes

| Lifetime | When does the container create a new instance? | Typical use cases |
|----------|-----------------------------------------------|-------------------|
| **Singleton** | Once per app lifetime | Configuration, logging, external clients that are thread‑safe. |
| **Scoped** | Once per request / scope | EF Core `DbContext`, per‑request business objects, `IHttpContextAccessor`. |
| **Transient** | Every time it’s injected | Lightweight stateless services, factory helpers. |

> **Key rule**: Do not store a scoped service in a singleton, otherwise you’ll get a *runtime error* (`IServiceScopeFactory` will complain).  

If you need a scoped service inside a singleton, inject `IServiceScopeFactory` and create a scope manually.

---

## 3. Injecting Services

### 3.1 Constructor Injection (the most common)

```csharp
public class UserController : Controller
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _repo;
    private readonly ILogger<UserController> _logger;

    public UserController(IUserRepository repo, ILogger<UserController> logger)
    {
        _repo = repo;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        var users = _repo.GetAll();
        return View(users);
    }
}
```

### 3.2 Action‑Method Injection (parameter injection)

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Register([FromBody] RegisterDto dto,
                              [FromServices] IEmailSender emailSender)
{
    // emailSender is resolved from DI
}
```

Add `[FromServices]` to signal DI; otherwise MVC treats the parameter as a model binder source.

### 3.3 Razor Pages & Blazor

```csharp
@page
@inject IUserRepository UserRepo
@inject ILogger<Page1> Logger

<h1>Users</h1>
@foreach (var user in UserRepo.GetAll())
{
    <p>@user.Name</p>
}
```

### 3.4 Minimal APIs (top‑level statements)

```csharp
app.MapGet("/weather", async (IWeatherService svc) =>
{
    var forecast = await svc.GetForecastAsync();
    return Results.Ok(forecast);
});
```

The framework automatically resolves the parameters for the delegate.

### 3.5 Middleware

```csharp
public class MyMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly IEmailSender _emailSender;

    public MyMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, IEmailSender emailSender)
    {
        _next = next;
        _emailSender = emailSender;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        // Use _emailSender
        await _next(context);
    }
}
```

Register with `app.UseMiddleware<MyMiddleware>();`

---

## 4. Options & Configuration

ASP.NET Core uses the *Options pattern* to bind configuration sections to POCO classes.

```csharp
public class StripeOptions
{
    public string ApiKey { get; set; }
    public string Secret { get; set; }
}
```

Register:

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<StripeOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("Stripe"));
```

Inject:

```csharp
public class PaymentService
{
    private readonly StripeOptions _opts;

    public PaymentService(IOptions<StripeOptions> opts)
    {
        _opts = opts.Value;     // read-only snapshot
    }
}
```

If you need to respond to changes at runtime, use `IOptionsMonitor<T>`.

---

## 5. Advanced Patterns

| Pattern | When & How |
|---------|------------|
| **Open Generic Types** | `services.AddScoped(typeof(IRepository<>), typeof(Repository<>));` |
| **Multiple Implementations** | Register several `IMyService` implementations; inject `IEnumerable<IMyService>`. |
| **Named/Keyed Services** | ASP.NET Core’s container does not support names directly; use factory or custom wrapper. |
| **Service Locator** | `var svc = app.Services.GetRequiredService<IService>();` – discouraged, use constructor injection. |
| **Custom Factory** | `services.AddTransient<IMyService>(sp => new MyService(sp.GetRequiredService<ILogger<MyService>>()));` |
| **Scope in Background Tasks** | `IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory = app.Services.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>();` <br> `using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();` <br> `var repo = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IUserRepository>();` |
| **Replacing the Default Container** | Pass a custom `IServiceProviderFactory` to `WebHostBuilder`. Common replacements: Autofac, StructureMap, Ninject. |

---

## 6. Testing with DI

*Unit tests* – Create a `ServiceCollection` and register the same services you use in production.

```csharp
var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddTransient<IUserRepository, FakeUserRepository>();
services.AddSingleton<ILogger<UserController>, NullLogger<UserController>>();

var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
var controller = new UserController(provider.GetRequiredService<IUserRepository>(),
                                   provider.GetRequiredService<ILogger<UserController>>());
```

*Integration tests* – Use `WebApplicationFactory<T>` which automatically sets up the DI container.

```csharp
public class UserControllerTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public UserControllerTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task GetUsers_ReturnsOk()
    {
        var response = await _client.GetAsync("/users");
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
    }
}
```

---

## 7. Common Pitfalls & Best Practices

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **Singleton holds a scoped service** | Inject `IServiceScopeFactory` instead, or change the service lifetime. |
| **Heavy work in constructors** | Do not perform async work; use lazy or factory patterns instead. |
| **Resolving services manually** (`app.Services.GetService`) | Prefer constructor injection. Manual resolution is fine for factory methods or background services. |
| **Injecting into static classes** | Avoid. Pass required services into instance methods or use `IHttpContextAccessor`. |
| **Multiple registrations of same service** | Last registration wins, but if you need multiple, inject `IEnumerable<T>`. |
| **Null values from `Configure<T>`** | Verify that the configuration section exists or use `GetRequiredSection`. |
| **Long‑lived context** | Do not keep EF Core `DbContext` beyond the request scope. |

---

## 8. Quick Reference

| API | What it does | Typical usage |
|-----|--------------|---------------|
| `builder.Services.AddSingleton<TService, TImpl>()` | Registers a singleton | Logging, configuration |
| `builder.Services.AddScoped<TService, TImpl>()` | Registers per request | DbContext, repositories |
| `builder.Services.AddTransient<TService, TImpl>()` | Registers transient | Helpers, adapters |
| `builder.Services.Configure<TOptions>(config)` | Binds config section to POCO | App settings |
| `builder.Services.AddHttpClient<TService, TImpl>()` | Registers HttpClient factory | External APIs |
| `services.GetRequiredService<T>()` | Resolved a service manually | Factories, background tasks |
| `app.UseMiddleware<TMiddleware>()` | Adds middleware that gets dependencies via constructor | Logging, auth, custom logic |
| `app.MapGet("/endpoint", (TService svc) => … )` | Minimal API endpoint with DI | Simple API routes |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Register** everything you need with `IServiceCollection` (`AddSingleton`, `AddScoped`, `AddTransient`, `Configure`, etc.).
2. ASP.NET Core will **build a single `IServiceProvider`** when the host starts.
3. **Inject** your services wherever the framework calls you (controllers, middleware, Razor Pages, minimal API handlers) using constructor or `[FromServices]` parameters.
4. Pick the right lifetime: singleton for long‑lived, scoped for per‑request, transient for stateless helpers.
5. For testing, build a `ServiceCollection` that mirrors your production registrations, or use `WebApplicationFactory`.

That’s the core of how dependency injection works in ASP.NET Core—simple, integrated, and powerful enough for almost every scenario. Happy coding!
